---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'components', label: 'Component testing' },
   { id: 'audits', label: 'WCAG audits' },
   { id: 'agents', label: 'AI agent automation' },
   { id: 'targets', label: 'Execution targets' },
   { id: 'next', label: 'Where to start' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Automated WCAG and screen reader testing"
   summary="<p><strong>a11ied</strong> scripts <abbr title='Apple&apos;s built-in screen reader for macOS'><a href='https://support.apple.com/guide/voiceover/welcome/mac' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer'>VoiceOver</a></abbr>, <abbr title='NonVisual Desktop Access: the open-source screen reader for Windows'><a href='https://www.nvaccess.org/' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer'>NVDA</a></abbr>, and a fast simulated <abbr title='Assistive technology software that speaks on-screen text and UI controls to blind and low-vision users'>screen reader</abbr>. It runs <abbr title='Open-source automated accessibility testing engine for HTML and DOM structure from Deque Systems'><a href='https://github.com/dequelabs/axe-core' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer'>axe-core</a></abbr> scans and reports each failure with the <abbr title='Web Content Accessibility Guidelines: the W3C standard of testable criteria for digital accessibility'><a href='https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer'>WCAG</a></abbr> success criterion it violates.</p><p>Test components during development, audit routes in CI, and let AI coding agents check what a screen reader actually announces. Drive it from the <code>a1</code> CLI, your Vitest suite, or an MCP server.</p>"
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="panel" id="components">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Development</p>
         <h2>Test components as you build</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         Static HTML linters check syntax, but cannot verify interactive behavior. When you
         develop a combobox, modal, or form, <code>a11ied</code> lets you write standard test
         assertions for keyboard focus, <abbr title="Accessible Rich Internet Applications: W3C specification of attributes that define accessible UI roles, states, and properties"><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/wai-aria/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">ARIA</a></abbr> state,
         and spoken announcements.
      </p>
      <pre><code>import {'{'} describe {'}'} from 'vitest';
import {'{'} test {'}'} from 'a11ied/vitest';

const combobox = test.extend({'{'}
   srOptions: {'{'} url: 'http://localhost:3000/components/combobox' {'}'},
{'}'});

describe('combobox component', () => {'{'}
   combobox('announces active option and expanded state', ({'{'} sr {'}'}) => {'{'}
      sr.press('Tab');
      sr.expectSpoken('combobox, Select country, collapsed');

      sr.press('ArrowDown');
      sr.expectSpoken('combobox, Select country, expanded');
      sr.expectSpoken('option, Canada, 1 of 3');
   {'}'});
{'}'});</code></pre>
      <p>
         This verifies <strong><abbr title="WCAG 4.1.2: Name, Role, Value requires that UI components have accessible names, roles, and states"><a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/name-role-value.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value)</a></abbr></strong> and <strong><abbr title="WCAG 2.4.3: Focus Order requires that interactive components receive focus in an order that preserves meaning and operability"><a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/focus-order.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2.4.3 (Focus Order)</a></abbr></strong> in your normal Vitest or Jest suite. In-process tests
         run against the simulated virtual screen reader in milliseconds, requiring zero extra
         system setup.
      </p>
      <p>
         Need to check expected keyboard chords or attributes for a custom widget? The CLI includes
         built-in reference data from the <abbr title="W3C ARIA Authoring Practices Guide: official design patterns and keyboard interaction standards for custom web widgets"><a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">W3C ARIA Authoring Practices Guide (APG)</a></abbr>:
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 pattern combobox</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="audits">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Auditing</p>
         <h2>Perform full-page WCAG audits</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         Run accessibility audits against rendered pages from your terminal or CI
         pipeline. <code>a1 audit</code> executes <abbr title="Open-source automated accessibility testing engine for HTML and DOM structure from Deque Systems"><a href="https://github.com/dequelabs/axe-core" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">axe-core</a></abbr> against
         the live DOM, maps every finding to numbered <abbr title="Web Content Accessibility Guidelines: the W3C standard of testable criteria for digital accessibility"><a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">WCAG</a></abbr> success
         criteria, and prints actionable remediation guidance.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 audit http://localhost:3000/checkout --level AA</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>&#10007; 2 problems to fix.

1.1.1  Non-text Content  [A]
  button[type="button"] > img
  Fix: Provide an alt attribute or aria-label for the icon button.

3.3.1  Error Identification  [A]
  input#card-number
  Fix: Reference the error message element using aria-describedby.</code></pre>
      <p>
         To understand which criteria apply to a given interface, run <code>a1 inspect</code>
         (see <a href="/relevant-criteria">relevant criteria</a>). To inspect full criterion details,
         techniques, and <a href="/test-methods">test methods</a> without leaving the terminal:
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 wcag 3.3.1</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="agents">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">AI Agents</p>
         <h2>Real screen reader automation for AI agents</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         Coding agents can write frontend markup, but they cannot wear headphones or listen to
         screen reader speech. Without a feedback loop, agents introduce accessibility
         regressions in dynamic components.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>a11ied</code> provides a local <abbr title="Model Context Protocol: open standard for connecting AI coding assistants to external tools"><a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Model Context Protocol (MCP)</a></abbr> server
         that gives Claude, Cursor, Windsurf, and other agents native tools to inspect accessibility
         trees, drive <abbr title="Apple's built-in screen reader for macOS"><a href="https://support.apple.com/guide/voiceover/welcome/mac" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">VoiceOver</a></abbr> or <abbr title="NonVisual Desktop Access: the open-source screen reader for Windows"><a href="https://www.nvaccess.org/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NVDA</a></abbr>,
         and verify fixes against <abbr title="Web Content Accessibility Guidelines: the W3C standard of testable criteria for digital accessibility"><a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">WCAG</a></abbr>.
      </p>
      <pre data-label="CONFIG"><code>{`{
  "mcpServers": {
    "a11ied": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "a11ied", "mcp"]
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>
      <p>
         Once connected, an agent can verify interactive workflows autonomously:
      </p>
      <pre data-label="AI AGENT PROMPT"><code>Review the checkout dialog on http://localhost:3000/checkout.
Start a screen reader session, open the modal, and verify:
1. Focus moves to the first field inside the dialog.
2. VoiceOver announces the dialog title and modal role upon opening.
3. Pressing Escape closes the dialog and restores focus to the trigger.</code></pre>
      <p>
         The agent drives the screen reader, inspects the spoken phrase log, verifies WCAG
         compliance, and adjusts the component before opening a pull request.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="targets">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Architecture</p>
            <h2>Execution targets: simulation vs. real OS</h2>
         </div>
         <p>
            <code>a11ied</code> supports two target modes to balance testing speed with real-world
            fidelity:
         </p>
         <div class="route-list">
            <div class="route-row">
               <strong>Virtual screen reader</strong>
               <span>
                  A fast, headless JavaScript implementation of screen reader navigation and ARIA
                  computation. Runs in milliseconds in Node.js or Linux CI containers with zero
                  system permissions. Ideal for unit tests, component tests, and pull request checks.
               </span>
            </div>
            <div class="route-row">
               <strong>VoiceOver & NVDA</strong>
               <span>
                  Real operating system assistive technologies driven via <abbr title="Open-source library for driving VoiceOver and NVDA with code"><a href="https://guidepup.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Guidepup</a></abbr>.
                  Automates AppleScript control on macOS and accessibility APIs on Windows.
                  Ideal for end-to-end integration tests, reproducing platform-specific quirks,
                  and release verification. See the <a href="/screen-readers">screen readers guide</a>.
               </span>
            </div>
         </div>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">Environment check</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            Run <code>a1 doctor</code> to check which browsers and screen readers are configured
            and ready on your current machine.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="feature-block" id="next">
      <h2>Where to start</h2>
      <div class="feature-grid">
         <section class="feature-card">
            <h3>Quickstart</h3>
            <p>Install the package, check your machine, and run your first virtual session in 2 minutes.</p>
            <p class="feature-link"><a href="/quickstart">Get started</a></p>
         </section>

         <section class="feature-card">
            <h3>Component testing</h3>
            <p>Write screen reader assertions with the <code>a11ied/vitest</code> fixture, or with <code>a11ied/test</code> under Jest or another runner.</p>
            <p class="feature-link"><a href="/guides/vitest">Vitest guide</a></p>
         </section>

         <section class="feature-card">
            <h3>AI agent setup</h3>
            <p>Connect the MCP server to Cursor, Claude, or Windsurf and start testing autonomously.</p>
            <p class="feature-link"><a href="/guides/agents">Agent guide</a></p>
         </section>

         <section class="feature-card">
            <h3>Interactive demo</h3>
            <p>Practice auditing and fixing real accessibility violations on a sample team settings page.</p>
            <p class="feature-link"><a href="/guides/demo">Practice on demo</a></p>
         </section>
      </div>
   </section>
</DocShell>
